From: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: hash offloaded flows across the 32 TX rings

airoha_dev_select_queue() picks the QDMA channel from port->id and the
queue inside that channel from skb->priority, so the ring a packet lands
on is a function of its egress port and its priority alone.  Every flow
leaving one port therefore shares eight of the 32 TX rings no matter how
many 5-tuples the box carries: one busy TCP stream and forty small ones
are enqueued on the same eight rings, and the other 24 stay idle.

Deriving the channel from the flow hash instead spreads unrelated flows
over the four HW QoS channels.  The queue inside the channel still
follows skb->priority, so DSCP/QoS classification keeps choosing among
the eight queues of whichever channel the flow landed on.  The ring
count, the queue mapping and the IRQ affinity are untouched: only which
of the 32 rings a packet joins changes.

ndo_select_queue() only ever sees packets the CPU transmits, so this
cannot be gated on "the flow is offloaded": a PPE-bound flow's packets
never reach the stack at all.  What the knob spreads is the traffic that
does cross the CPU - flows before they are offloaded, and flows the PPE
declined.

The knob is off by default and stays inert while any QoS channel of the
QDMA is claimed by an HTB leaf: the channels are a shared per-QDMA
resource, and a hashed packet on a shaped channel would be scheduled by
another port's shaper.  In that state the port-derived channel is used
as before, so the knob can never silently bypass or misapply a
configured rate.

A packet with no 5-tuple keeps the port-derived channel too, and is
counted: landing it on ring 0 would move every ARP frame of every port
onto port 0's channel.

It is a 0444 parameter - set on the kernel command line or at insmod -
since the channel binding is a property of the device's lifetime, not
something to flip under flows that are already enqueued.

Getting the distribution from userspace needs a counter, and ethtool -S
is the wrong shape for it: the rings belong to a QDMA block that several
net_devices share, so per-netdev statistics would report the same 32
rings five times.  qdma/tx_ring_hist reports them once, where they are
accounted, and is only filled while the knob is on.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c | 124 ++++++++++++++++++++++-
 drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h |   6 ++
 2 files changed, 127 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
@@ -660,6 +660,21 @@
 	struct airoha_queue q_rx[AIROHA_NUM_RX_RING];
 
 	DECLARE_BITMAP(qos_channel_map, AIROHA_NUM_QOS_CHANNELS);
+
+	/* Packets enqueued on each TX ring, filled only while tx_ring_hash is
+	 * on.  Every enqueue takes the ring's own q->lock, so a plain u64
+	 * increment from airoha_dev_xmit() has a single writer per entry and
+	 * needs no seqcount.
+	 */
+	struct {
+		u64 pkts;
+		u64 bytes;
+	} tx_ring_stat[AIROHA_NUM_TX_RING];
+
+	/* Packets tx_ring_hash could not classify and that kept the
+	 * port-derived channel.
+	 */
+	u64 tx_ring_hash_fallback;
 };
 
 enum airoha_dev_flags {
